\subsection{import_blocks()}{

Copy or type the pages from the screen of the server into
\verb{<version>_results/real/export_blocks.txt}. \code{import_blocks()} reads that
file. It removes the trailing white space of each line and each empty
line, and it ignores the lines before the first header line. The lines
after a header line, up to the next header line, are the text of that
page.

\code{import_blocks()} stops and writes nothing when:
\itemize{
\item the file has no header line,
\item a header line names another version, or the pages name more than one
run date or more than one count of blocks,
\item some header lines end with the token \code{ fake} and some do not,
\item the md5 of the text of a page differs from its header, and the error
names the block and the page,
\item a page is missing, and the error names the block and the page,
\item a page comes twice with other text. A page that comes twice with the
same text counts once.
\item R cannot parse the text of a block, or the text is not a list of one
named value.
}

It rebuilds each value with \code{eval(parse(text = text), envir = baseenv())}.
So import only the pages that \code{export_blocks()} printed, because
\code{import_blocks()} runs the file as R code. It writes the named list of
the values to \verb{<version>_results/real/blocks.qs2} and overwrites that
file. It writes there in a one-file project too, where the scenario
\code{real} of this computer is in \verb{fake/}, see \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}. When
the pages end with the token \code{ fake}, the list has the attribute
\code{pbn_fake = TRUE}.
