\subsection{read_blocks()}{

\code{read_blocks()} returns \verb{<version>_results/real/blocks.qs2} when it
exists. Else it returns the \code{blocks.qs2} of the scenario \code{real} of this
computer, and it gives a warning that the numbers are fake. In a one-file
project that file is \verb{<version>_results/fake/blocks.qs2}. In a project
that is not one-file it is \code{real/blocks.qs2} itself, so \code{read_blocks()}
stops. \code{\link[=txt]{txt()}} and \code{\link[=make_checklists]{make_checklists()}} read the blocks in the same way.
The rule \code{built_reads_source} of \code{\link[=check_project]{check_project()}} says which functions
a manuscript in \verb{manuscript/built/} MUST use.

The values are fake when they come from another file than
\code{real/blocks.qs2}, or when \code{real/blocks.qs2} has \code{pbn_fake = TRUE}. For
fake values in a document that knitr renders, such as a \code{.qmd} or an
\code{.Rmd}, \code{read_blocks()}, \code{\link[=txt]{txt()}} and \code{\link[=make_checklists]{make_checklists()}} add a knitr
document hook. The hook puts the line \verb{**FAKE DATA**} once at the top of
the output, after the YAML header. A document that reads only real
values gets no such line.
